# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_heap *vx_heap_new(size_t reserve);
bool vx_heap_failed(const vx_heap *h);
void *vx_heap_alloc(vx_heap *h, size_t n);
void *vx_heap_alloc_aligned(vx_heap *h, size_t n, size_t align);
void vx_heap_free(vx_heap *h, void *p);
size_t vx_heap_usable(const vx_heap *h, const void *p);
vx_heap *vx_heap_process(void);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A heap is `libvx`'s third kind of memory, after arenas and pools, for objects whose lifetimes have nothing to do with each other (09 §4.3, M6 step 6e1e2b, os-requirements R6).
There is no global `malloc`: a heap is an object, made and passed explicitly.
`vx_heap_process` is the one a program keeps as its global: the native C library's `malloc` family sits on it (ADR-0033 §1), as does the Swift runtime.
First-party system code keeps to arenas and pools.

`vx_heap_new` makes a heap of up to <reserve> bytes, rounded up to 256 MiB and at most 16 GiB.
That is address space, reserved (as(2)); memory comes as the heap is used, from lazy VMOs (vmo(2), ADR-0046) mapped into it 256 MiB at a time.
If it cannot be made it returns the nil heap, which gives nothing and takes nothing: `vx_heap_failed` says which, and a program checks once, where it matters.

`vx_heap_alloc` returns at least <n> bytes aligned to 16, and `vx_heap_alloc_aligned` at least <n> aligned to <align>, a power of two up to 64 KiB; both return `nullptr` when there is no room, or for a bad alignment.
The memory is not cleared, though a block of pages the heap has given back reads as zeros.

`vx_heap_free` gives a block back; `nullptr` is nothing.
A pointer that is not the start of one of the heap's blocks ends the program, as a fault does; freeing a block twice is not always caught.

`vx_heap_usable` is the size of <p>'s block, at least what was asked for: the caller may use all of it and grow into it, as Swift's arrays and strings do.
It is 0 for anything that is not one of the heap's blocks.

A block of up to 32 KiB comes from a slab of its size class, 64 KiB of blocks of one size, four classes to each doubling; each class has its own lock, so threads asking for different sizes do not wait on each other.
A larger block is a run of 64 KiB spans.
Free runs are joined with their neighbours, and once more than 4 MiB of them may still hold pages, their pages go back to the system (`VX_VMO_DECOMMIT`); so do slabs left empty, all but one of each class.

# EXAMPLES

A program's own heap for an editor's buffers:

```c
static vx_heap *buffers;

buffers = vx_heap_new(1ull << 30);
if (vx_heap_failed(buffers)) return "no memory";
char *line = vx_heap_alloc(buffers, 200);
size_t room = vx_heap_usable(buffers, line); // 224
vx_heap_free(buffers, line);
```

